      "title": "Residential Experience Specialist",
      "description": "Residential Experience Specialist - Marquette, Michigan, United States Residential Experience Specialist - Marquette, Michigan, United States Skip to Main Content Northern Michigan University Careers Kiosk mode Residential Experience Specialist 495141 Marquette, Michigan, 49855 Housing & Residence Life Staff Closing on: Oct 29 2026 Position Title: Residential Experience Specialist Department: Housing & Residence Life Term of Assignment: Continuing Term End Date, If Applicable: FLSA Status: Non-Exempt = overtime eligible Work Schedule: Traditionally Monday-Friday, 8:00 am to 5:00 pm with occasional evening and weekend work required. Salary: Targeted Salary Range: $42,000-$45,000 Travel: No travel Brief Statement of Duties Brief Statement of Duties: Serve as a key professional resource within Housing and Residence Life by providing guidance on housing policies, contracts, billing, and operational processes while supporting students, families, and campus partners. Address complex concerns, coordinate communication and housing operations, support major residential transitions and housing cycles, and contribute to initiatives that enhance service quality and the overall residential experience. Minimum Qualifications Required Education: Bachelor's Degree Discipline/Degree Area: Required Specialized Training/Certifications: Required Minimum Work Experience: a) One year of professional experience in housing and residence life, higher education student affairs, or a related higher education administrative setting; and an additional b) One year of experience working directly with the public in a customer service-focused role that involved addressing concerns, providing guidance and resources, and resolving issues in a professional manner. Knowledge, Skills, Abilities, or Attributes Required for Satisfactory Performance of the Position Duties: Excellent interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills with the ability to effectively navigate sensitive or challenging situations involving students, families, and campus stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to maintain confidentiality, exercise sound professional judgment, and appropriately manage sensitive student information and complex situations. Strong organizational, critical thinking and project management skills with the ability to prioritize multiple responsibilities, balance competing demands, and adapt to changing operational needs. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a fast-paced environment while maintaining professionalism, accuracy, and responsiveness. Additional Desirable Qualifications Additional Desirable Qualifications: Experience using housing management systems such as StarRez or THD. Experience with financial or purchasing systems such as Concur. Experience in communications, marketing, recruitment, or public relations. Experience working within a Public Private Partnership (P3) environment involving contractual partnerships, shared operational responsibilities, and coordination between organizations. Experience supporting college admissions, new student/transfer orientation, housing operations, or student services initiatives within higher education. Other Special Instructions to Applicants: Submit application, cover letter, resume and references. Contact Information: Erica Jean, HR Generalist, 906-227-1087 NMU is an equal opportunity employer. Apply Now! Recently Posted Jobs Residential Experience Specialist Housing & Residence Life Serve as a key professional resource within Housing and Residence Life by providing guidance on housing policies, contracts, billing, and operational processes while supporting students, families, and campus partners. Address complex concerns, coordinate communication and housing operations, support major residential transitions and housing cycles, and contribute to initiatives that enhance service quality and the overall residential experience. Economics/Assistant Professor/Three Year Term Economics Teach courses in Money and Banking, Intermediate Macroeconomics, Principles of Macroeconomics, and be prepared to teach Introduction to Economics and Principles of Microeconomics. The normal teaching load is three courses (12 credit hours) per semester, with up to three different course preparations per semester. Additional responsibilities include service at the department, college, and university levels, and student advising. Resources are available for engaging in professional development, research, and conference travel. Desirable qualifications: Being able to teach Comparative Economic Systems and American Economic History. Being able to integrate emerging technologies, including AI, into teaching and other professional activities in innovative and effective ways. This position is campus-based and is not currently eligible for sponsorship. Earth, Environmental, and Geographical Sciences/Assistant Professor in Environmental Hazards/Tenure Track Earth, Env, & Geog Sciences The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses that complement and strengthen the Department’s curriculum, maintain an active and student-engaged research program in environmental hazards and applied GIS or a closely related field, publish in peer-reviewed outlets, and pursue external funding opportunities. Additional responsibilities include advising undergraduate and graduate students and contributing to departmental, university, and professional service. This position is not available for sponsorship. This is a campus-based position. Manager-Grant Accounting/Accounting Services Controllers Office Plan, develop and refine operating procedures and computerized systems for financial reporting. Monitor, review and document University financial transactions for compliance with accounting principles, University policies, federal circulars and Internal Revenue Service rules and regulations. Prepare and/or assist with preparation of many of the University's financial statements, annual audits, income tax return, revenue projections of University endowments and expenditure projections for special projects. Responsible for all post award financial management functions, including expenditure monitoring, financial reporting, grant close-out, and audit. Biology - Forensics/Assistant Professor/Tenure Track Biology The Tenure Track Assistant Professor position requires a Ph.D. in forensic biology or a related field. Ph.D must be earned by August 15, 2027. The assignments will include teaching a range of courses related to forensic science such as Introduction to Forensic Science, Forensic Biology, Forensic Microscopy, Forensic Entomology, other areas of forensic science, a career seminar, or advanced courses in areas of specialty. Assignments may also include teaching in related fields including human anatomy & physiology. The selected candidate will be expected to develop a research program in forensic biology incorporating undergraduate and graduate students. Preference will be given to those applicants whose work engages decomposition ecology or related specialties in entomology, microbiology, morphological decomposition, and postmortem interval estimation. This is a campus based position. This position is not currently available for sponsorship. Temporary Production Cook Dining Services-Central Office This is a posting for part-time temporary positions which are used to supplement and augment the regular NMU workforce. This position is primarily responsible for food preparation and cooking. The length of hire might vary from 1 day to 4 months. Sign up for job alerts By signing up for job alerts, you will be notified of vacancies based on your career category selection. You are able to sign up for as many career categories as you would like. Job Alert ea89b9bc Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the l